1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,385
Why do your glasses get foggy

2
00:00:01,385 --> 00:00:02,537
coming in from the cold?!

3
00:00:02,717 --> 00:00:05,007
Professor Poppleton here! Let's POP the mystery!

4
00:00:05,187 --> 00:00:06,847
Here's the weird part. Walk back

5
00:00:06,847 --> 00:00:08,871
outside, and they stay perfectly clear!

6
00:00:09,051 --> 00:00:11,000
About 64% of US adults wear

7
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,383
glasses or contacts. If yours are

8
00:00:13,383 --> 00:00:15,333
glasses, you know this fog!

9
00:00:15,963 --> 00:00:18,673
By our maths, in a 70°F

10
00:00:18,673 --> 00:00:22,207
room at 40% humidity, any lens

11
00:00:22,207 --> 00:00:24,799
below about 45°F fogs.

12
00:00:25,379 --> 00:00:27,684
Today's experiment: the hidden water, the

13
00:00:27,684 --> 00:00:29,426
fog line, and the one-way door.

14
00:00:29,606 --> 00:00:31,589
Three clues, one very misty

15
00:00:31,589 --> 00:00:33,352
mystery. Let's crack it!

16
00:00:33,932 --> 00:00:35,656
Guess first! Where does the fog

17
00:00:35,656 --> 00:00:37,325
on your glasses come from? The

18
00:00:37,325 --> 00:00:39,104
lens itself, water in the room's

19
00:00:39,104 --> 00:00:40,105
air, or your eyes?

20
00:00:43,205 --> 00:00:44,517
Lock in your answer. We'll

21
00:00:44,517 --> 00:00:45,526
test it in a moment.

22
00:00:46,006 --> 00:00:48,121
So, experiment one. Look around your

23
00:00:48,121 --> 00:00:49,942
room. It seems bone dry, right?

24
00:00:50,122 --> 00:00:51,373
But the air is full of

25
00:00:51,373 --> 00:00:53,078
water you can't see. It floats

26
00:00:53,078 --> 00:00:54,386
around as water vapour.

27
00:00:54,566 --> 00:00:56,463
Water vapour is a gas. Each

28
00:00:56,463 --> 00:00:58,711
tiny molecule is one oxygen atom

29
00:00:58,711 --> 00:01:00,327
and two hydrogen atoms.

30
00:01:00,507 --> 00:01:02,200
Warm air can carry lots of

31
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:03,763
it. By our maths, air at

32
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:05,195
70°F can hold up to 18

33
00:01:05,195 --> 00:01:06,628
grams per cubic metre.

34
00:01:06,808 --> 00:01:08,710
But cold air holds far less.

35
00:01:08,710 --> 00:01:10,612
Chill the air, and the extra

36
00:01:10,612 --> 00:01:12,447
vapour has to go somewhere.

37
00:01:12,627 --> 00:01:14,536
So it gathers into tiny liquid

38
00:01:14,536 --> 00:01:16,827
droplets, right on the coldest thing

39
00:01:16,827 --> 00:01:18,482
around. Like your glasses!

40
00:01:19,262 --> 00:01:21,088
But why does only the lens fog,

41
00:01:21,088 --> 00:01:23,150
and not the whole room? Because the

42
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:24,505
lens is the cold thing.

43
00:01:24,685 --> 00:01:26,322
Every patch of air has a fog

44
00:01:26,322 --> 00:01:28,603
line. Scientists call it the dew point.

45
00:01:28,783 --> 00:01:30,635
Cool air below its dew point, and

46
00:01:30,635 --> 00:01:33,272
its vapour starts turning into liquid droplets.

47
00:01:33,452 --> 00:01:35,265
In a 70°F room at 40%

48
00:01:35,265 --> 00:01:38,028
humidity, the dew point is about

49
00:01:38,028 --> 00:01:39,669
45°F, by our maths.

50
00:01:39,849 --> 00:01:41,562
A drier room has a lower

51
00:01:41,562 --> 00:01:43,845
line. At 30% humidity it's about

52
00:01:43,845 --> 00:01:45,629
37°F. At 50%, about 50°F.

53
00:01:45,809 --> 00:01:48,061
Now your glasses walk in from 32°F

54
00:01:48,061 --> 00:01:50,246
air. They're way below that line!

55
00:01:50,876 --> 00:01:53,495
The air touching the lens chills below

56
00:01:53,495 --> 00:01:55,839
its dew point, and drops its water

57
00:01:55,839 --> 00:01:57,631
right onto the glass. Fog!

58
00:01:57,811 --> 00:01:59,225
Oh dear. Now I can't even

59
00:01:59,225 --> 00:02:00,809
see my own magnificent hair!

60
00:02:01,689 --> 00:02:03,405
Remember your guess? Where does

61
00:02:03,405 --> 00:02:04,788
the fog really come from?

62
00:02:05,418 --> 00:02:07,255
Water in the room's air! Not

63
00:02:07,255 --> 00:02:09,091
the lens, and not your eyes.

64
00:02:09,271 --> 00:02:10,914
That water was floating around

65
00:02:10,914 --> 00:02:12,228
you the whole time. Your

66
00:02:12,228 --> 00:02:13,762
cold glasses just caught it.

67
00:02:13,942 --> 00:02:15,786
Click on subscribe, so your brain

68
00:02:15,786 --> 00:02:17,015
can be as big as mine!

69
00:02:17,795 --> 00:02:19,421
But here's the twist. Why don't

70
00:02:19,421 --> 00:02:20,837
they fog when you walk back

71
00:02:20,837 --> 00:02:21,782
out into the cold?

72
00:02:21,962 --> 00:02:24,345
Inside, your glasses warmed

73
00:02:24,345 --> 00:02:25,846
up to about 70°F.

74
00:02:26,026 --> 00:02:28,285
Now think about the air outside.

75
00:02:28,285 --> 00:02:30,261
At 32°F, its dew point can't

76
00:02:30,261 --> 00:02:31,673
be higher than 32°F.

77
00:02:31,853 --> 00:02:34,036
Air can never be more than 100%

78
00:02:34,036 --> 00:02:36,431
humid. So its fog line always sits

79
00:02:36,431 --> 00:02:38,684
at or below its own temperature.

80
00:02:38,864 --> 00:02:40,625
Your warm lens is far above

81
00:02:40,625 --> 00:02:43,495
that line. Nothing condenses. Crystal clear!

82
00:02:43,675 --> 00:02:45,178
But breathe on them, and they

83
00:02:45,178 --> 00:02:46,733
fog right away. Your breath is

84
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:48,495
warm and nearly soaked with water.

85
00:02:48,675 --> 00:02:50,906
In lab tests, breath came out

86
00:02:50,906 --> 00:02:54,215
at about 93°F, almost completely saturated.

87
00:02:54,215 --> 00:02:56,138
Its fog line is sky-high!

88
00:02:56,318 --> 00:02:57,966
Same with a steaming mug.

89
00:02:57,966 --> 00:02:59,351
Warm, wet air meets a

90
00:02:59,351 --> 00:03:01,263
cooler lens, and fog appears.

91
00:03:02,043 --> 00:03:04,525
POP QUIZ! In summer, you leave

92
00:03:04,525 --> 00:03:07,504
a 72°F air-conditioned room for 90°F

93
00:03:07,504 --> 00:03:10,234
air at 70% humidity. Your glasses

94
00:03:10,234 --> 00:03:12,717
stay clear, fog up, or freeze?

95
00:03:16,167 --> 00:03:18,073
They fog up! In summer,

96
00:03:18,073 --> 00:03:19,979
the one-way door flips.

97
00:03:20,159 --> 00:03:22,723
By our maths, that muggy air

98
00:03:22,723 --> 00:03:25,105
has a dew point near 79°F.

99
00:03:25,105 --> 00:03:27,761
Your 72°F lens sits below it!

100
00:03:28,541 --> 00:03:31,139
Science deep dive! Just how much water

101
00:03:31,139 --> 00:03:33,121
is hiding in your room's air?

102
00:03:33,301 --> 00:03:35,126
Take a 12 by 12 foot bedroom

103
00:03:35,126 --> 00:03:37,472
with an 8 foot ceiling. That's about

104
00:03:37,472 --> 00:03:38,970
33 cubic metres of air.

105
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:41,386
At 70°F and 40% humidity,

106
00:03:41,386 --> 00:03:44,069
each cubic metre carries about

107
00:03:44,069 --> 00:03:46,484
7 grams of invisible water.

108
00:03:47,114 --> 00:03:49,048
Add it up, and your bedroom

109
00:03:49,048 --> 00:03:50,767
air holds about 1 cup of

110
00:03:50,767 --> 00:03:52,200
water, by our maths!

111
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:54,115
A whole cup, floating around you

112
00:03:54,115 --> 00:03:55,579
as a gas! Your cold glasses

113
00:03:55,579 --> 00:03:56,718
just grab a tiny sip.

114
00:03:56,898 --> 00:03:59,507
Steady, Poppleton, steady... no, I'm too excited!

115
00:04:00,487 --> 00:04:01,440
So why does fog look

116
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:02,631
white, if water is clear?

117
00:04:02,811 --> 00:04:04,663
Because fog is a crowd of tiny

118
00:04:04,663 --> 00:04:07,194
droplets. Each one bends and scatters the

119
00:04:07,194 --> 00:04:09,107
light, so the lens turns milky.

120
00:04:09,287 --> 00:04:11,550
Anti-fog coatings play a clever trick.

121
00:04:11,550 --> 00:04:13,337
They make the water spread out

122
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:15,124
flat, in one thin, clear film.

123
00:04:15,304 --> 00:04:18,203
On those super water-loving surfaces, a drop

124
00:04:18,203 --> 00:04:20,311
meets the lens at an angle under

125
00:04:20,311 --> 00:04:22,354
10°. It barely beads up at all.

126
00:04:22,534 --> 00:04:24,825
And with no coating, the fog fades

127
00:04:24,825 --> 00:04:26,913
on its own. The lens warms past

128
00:04:26,913 --> 00:04:29,676
the fog line, and the droplets evaporate.

129
00:04:30,456 --> 00:04:31,619
So here's the whole machine.

130
00:04:31,799 --> 00:04:33,359
A cold lens chills the air

131
00:04:33,359 --> 00:04:35,338
touching it, until that air drops

132
00:04:35,338 --> 00:04:36,538
below its dew point.

133
00:04:36,718 --> 00:04:38,773
The vapour turns to droplets on the

134
00:04:38,773 --> 00:04:41,297
glass, and the droplets scatter light. Fog!

135
00:04:42,077 --> 00:04:43,648
THE BIG POP: your glasses fog

136
00:04:43,648 --> 00:04:45,653
coming in because they're colder than

137
00:04:45,653 --> 00:04:47,333
the room's fog line. The room's

138
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:48,633
own water lands on them.

139
00:04:48,813 --> 00:04:50,865
Going out, your warm lenses sit

140
00:04:50,865 --> 00:04:52,850
above the cold air's fog line.

141
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:54,108
So they stay clear.

142
00:04:54,288 --> 00:04:56,447
The water was never in your glasses.

143
00:04:56,447 --> 00:04:58,127
It was in the air all along.

144
00:04:59,307 --> 00:05:01,391
Where do your glasses fog the worst?

145
00:05:01,391 --> 00:05:03,128
The front door, the oven, or a

146
00:05:03,128 --> 00:05:05,328
steaming mug? Tell me in the comments.

147
00:05:05,508 --> 00:05:07,038
Want to see why the cold makes

148
00:05:07,038 --> 00:05:08,924
your phone shut off with charge left?

149
00:05:08,924 --> 00:05:10,709
That experiment file is right here.

150
00:05:10,889 --> 00:05:13,017
A new science mystery every week.

151
00:05:13,197 --> 00:05:15,562
Stay curious, and keep asking why!

